In Table [1](#summary) we present derived values for the volume emission measures of HII, HeII and HeIII (eq. [\[SdVtoEM\]](#SdVtoEM)), the production rate of EUV photons $Q_0:$ $h\nu>h\nu_0=13.6$ eV, $Q_1:$ $h\nu>h\nu_1=24.6$ eV, $Q_2:$ $h\nu>h\nu_2=54.4$ eV (eq. [\[eq:EMtoQ\]](#eq:EMtoQ)) and the inferred parameters of the underlying EUV spectra $L_\nu,$ $\gamma$ and $\gamma_2$ (eq. [\[eq:lnu\]](#eq:lnu)) for each extraction region of G330.9536, G332.8254, Sgr B2(M) and G328.8076 (Figures [1](#fig:sources) and [3](#fig:regions)). We see that for all extraction regions the specific luminosity of EUV for $13.6 \, \mathrm{eV}\leq h \nu < 54.4 \, \mathrm{eV}$ scales close to $L_\nu \sim \nu^{-4.5 \pm 0.4},$ and steepens even more for higher energies $h \nu \geq 54.4$ eV to $\sim \nu^{-\gamma_2},$ where $\gamma_2>15$. The value of the high energy spectral slope is dominated by G330.9536 which has lower molecular line content and thus allows for much more stringent limit on $[S\Delta V]_\mathrm{He^+48\alpha }$ and thus on $\gamma_2.$

Above we quote averaged values of the exponents over the non-overlapping extraction regions of the observed HII regions excluding G328.8076. The spectral slope $\gamma$ obtained for G328.8076 is $\sim 7.5$. However, the presence of strong molecular line features near He30$\alpha$ in its spectrum leads to large uncertainties and makes this estimate the least reliable. So we exclude this HII region from the calculation of $\langle \gamma \rangle$ and the following discussion. The 1 sigma uncertainties of the averaged spectral slope $\langle \gamma \rangle$ quoted above are dominated by the uncertainties of the model assumptions and not the standard deviation due to the averaging.

We compare this observational result with model spectra of an instantaneous $10^3 M_{\odot}$ starburst obtained with (i) Starburst99 v7.0.1 (cite: 1999ApJS..123....3L; 2014ApJS..212...14L) which assumes that all stars evolve as single stars only and (ii) the Binary Population and Spectral Synthesis code (BPASS) v2.2.1 (cite: 2017PASA...34...58E; Stanway18) which can take into account binary population and the interactions between the stars such as common envelope evolution and mass transfer. The BPASS can be set to evolve all stars as single stars, i.e. with binary interaction switched off. For illustration we present spectra produced by the two codes at 7.9 Myrs (Figure [4](#fig:models)). Left panel shows the spectra produced by BPASS with binary interaction included. The right panel show the spectra for single star evolution models produced by BPASS with binary interaction switched off and Starburst99 with the six evolutionary tracks – Geneva track with zero rotation (v00), Geneva tracks with $V=0.4$ rotation (v40), Geneva tracks with standards mass loss, Geneva tracks with high mass loss, original Padova tracks, Padova tracks with AGB stars. For description and the appropriate references see (cite: 2014ApJS..212...14L). In all cases the metallicity is set to $Z=0.02,$ except for Starburst 99’s Geneva v00 and v40 tracks for which only $Z=0.014$ is available. We scale the simulations to $10^3 M_{\odot}$ so that the production rate of $h\nu > 13.6 \, \mathrm{eV}$ photons ($Q_0$) predicted by the models is similar to the observed values for our extraction regions (see left panel in Figure [5](#fig:Qs.gammas) and Table [1](#summary)). The time range on the figure is set to span the lifetime of a single OB star, which is $< 30$ Myrs.

