# Ionizing photon count, volume emission measures, and EUV spectra

We use standard notation for the production rates of the ionizing photons above the ionization thresholds of HI, HeI and HeII, see eq. [\[Qs\]](#Qs). We assume that EUV spectrum in SF regions can be represented by broken power-law $$\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq.app:lnu}
 L_\nu = \left\{
 \begin{array}{ll}    
 \displaystyle L_0 (\nu/\nu_0)^{-\gamma},    & \quad \nu_0 \leq \nu < \nu_2\\
 \displaystyle L_2 (\nu/\nu_2)^{-\gamma_2},& \quad \nu \geq \nu_2.
 \end{array}\right.
\end{eqnarray}$$ Here $L_0, \, \gamma, \, L_2$ and $\gamma_2$ are constants, and $L_2= L_0 (\nu_2/\nu_0)^{-\gamma}$ to satisfy continuity at $\nu=\nu_2.$ Substituting relations ([\[eq.app:lnu\]](#eq.app:lnu)) into eq. [\[Qs\]](#Qs) we have $$\begin{eqnarray}
    && Q_0 = \frac{L_0/h}{\gamma} \left[ 1- \left(\frac{\nu_2}{\nu_0}\right)^{-\gamma} \right] +Q_2, \qquad 
    \label{eq:QCa} Q_1 = \frac{L_0/h}{\gamma} \left[ \left(\frac{\nu_1}{\nu_0}\right)^{-\gamma}- \left(\frac{\nu_2}{\nu_0}\right)^{-\gamma} \right] +Q_2, \\
    \nonumber && Q_2 = \frac{L_0/h}{\gamma_2} \left(\frac{\nu_2}{\nu_0}\right)^{-\gamma}.
\end{eqnarray}$$ We can solve this system numerically for $C,$ $\gamma$ and $\gamma_2.$ The solution can be written analytically if we make used of the fact that $Q_2 \ll Q_0$ and $Q_2 \ll Q_1.$ We get $$\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq:Caa2}
    \frac{Q_1}{Q_0} \simeq \frac{\nu_1^{-\gamma}-\nu_2^{-\gamma}}{\nu_0^{-\gamma}-\nu_2^{-\gamma}}, \qquad 
    L_0/h \simeq \frac{\gamma Q_0}{1-(\nu_2/\nu_0)^{-\gamma}}, \qquad
    \gamma_2 = \frac{L_0/h - \gamma Q_0 }{Q_2} = \gamma \frac{Q_0}{Q_2} \frac{1}{(\nu_2/\nu_0)^{\gamma}-1}.
\end{eqnarray}$$

To derive the ionizing photon production rates Q from the observed EM’s we assume (i) that the gas is in a radiation equilibrium, so that the number of recombinations of each ion species is equal to the number of ionizations producing this ion species per unit time and (ii) that all ionizing photons are used within the region and none escapes. Additionally, in order to obtain analytical solutions, (iii) we going to disregard the possibility that high energy photons can be stolen by species with lower ionizing threshold (for detailed treatment see e.g. (cite: ost06)). Thus we have: $$\begin{eqnarray}
    Q_0\simeq \alpha ^\mathrm{HI}_B {\rm EM}_\mathrm{HII}, \qquad
    Q_1\simeq \alpha ^\mathrm{HeI}_B {\rm EM}_\mathrm{HeII}, \qquad \label{eq:QEM}
    Q_2 \simeq \alpha ^\mathrm{HeII}_B {\rm EM}_\mathrm{HeIII},
\end{eqnarray}$$ where $$\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq:aBs}
  \displaystyle\alpha _B^\mathrm{HI}   = 2.59 \times 10^{-13} \, \mathrm{cm}^3 \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad  &
  \displaystyle\alpha _B^\mathrm{HeI} = 2.72 \times 10^{-13} \, \mathrm{cm}^3 \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad &
  \displaystyle\alpha _B^\mathrm{HeII}= 1.85 \times 10^{-12} \, \mathrm{cm}^3 \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{eqnarray}$$ are recombination coefficients of HI, HeI and HeII to all $\mathrm{n}\geq 2$ at $T=10^4$K.

Combining eqs ([\[eq:QEM\]](#eq:QEM)) and ([\[eq:Caa2\]](#eq:Caa2)) we get $$\begin{eqnarray}
    && \label{a} \frac{2.21^{\gamma}-1}{4^{\gamma}-1} \simeq 0.105 \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_{\rm HeII}}{10^{60} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right) \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_{\rm HII}} {10^{61} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right)^{-1}\\
    && L_0 \simeq 1.716 \times 10^{22} \frac{{\rm erg}}{\mathrm{s}\, \mathrm{Hz}} \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_{\rm HII}}{10^{61} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right) \frac{\gamma}{1-0.25^{\gamma}}\\
    && \label{a_2} \gamma_2 \simeq 1.4 \gamma \times 10^{2} \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_{\rm HII}}{10^{61} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right) \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_\mathrm{HeIII}}{10^{58} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right)^{-1} \left[4^{\gamma}-1\right]^{-1}\\
    && L_2 = 1.716 \times 10^{22} \frac{{\rm erg}}{\mathrm{s}\, \mathrm{Hz}}  \left(\frac{{\rm EM}_{\rm HII}}{10^{61} \, \mathrm{cm}^{-3}}\right) \frac{\gamma}{4^\gamma-1}.
\end{eqnarray}$$ Using eq. [\[a\]](#a) we can solve for $\gamma$, which we then substitute it into the rest of the equations to find $L_0, \, L_2$ and $\gamma_2,$ thus obtaining the intrinsic EUV spectra inside the region.

